%S A401333 0,10,11,17,20,30,31,37,40,50,51,57,60,70,71,77,80,90,91,97,100,110,
%T A401333 111,117,120,130,131,137,140,150,151,157,160,170,171,177,180,190,191,
%U A401333 197,200,210,211,217,220,230,231,237,240,250,251,257,260,270,271,277,280
%N A401333 Numbers k such that the units digit of 5*k equals the tens digit of k^5.
%C A401333 The sequence is periodic modulo 20, with least period 20. The four qualifying residue classes are {0, 10, 11, 17} (mod 20).
%C A401333 Therefore, exactly 4 out of every 20 consecutive nonnegative integers satisfy the defining condition, giving a natural density of 4/20 = 1/5 = 20%. These results follow from the complete classification of the residue classes.
%C A401333 This sequence is the case k = 5 of the generalized family defined by floor(n^k/10) mod 10 = (k*n) mod 10, extending A399563 (k = 2). The corresponding case k = 3 is A401332.
%F A401333 The terms are the nonnegative integers k satisfying floor(k^5/10) mod 10 = (5*k) mod 10.
%F A401333 Equivalently, k mod 20 is in {0, 10, 11, 17}.
%F A401333 The sequence has least period 20, so a(n+4) = a(n) + 20 for n >= 1.
%e A401333 For n = 11, 11^5 = 161051 (tens digit 5) and 5*11 = 55 (units digit 5), so 11 is in the sequence.
%e A401333 For n = 17, 17^5 = 1419857 (tens digit 5) and 5*17 = 85 (units digit 5), so 17 is in the sequence.
%e A401333 For n = 12, 12^5 = 248832 (tens digit 3) and 5*12 = 60 (units digit 0), so 12 is not in the sequence.
%t A401333 Select[Range[0, 500], Function[n, Mod[Quotient[n^5, 10], 10] == Mod[5*n, 10]]]
%o A401333 (Python) [n for n in range(501) if (n**5 // 10) % 10 == (5*n) % 10]
%Y A401333 Cf. A399563 (k = 2), A401332 (k = 3).
%K A401333 nonn,base,easy,new
%O A401333 1,2
%A A401333 _Yu-Wen Wang_ and Gwo-Haur Hwang, Oct 21 2026
